<div class="path-head"><p class="featured-region">Reading path · 10 stops</p><h1 class="path-title">The Living Peoples</h1><p class="path-tagline">Ten Indigenous peoples alive today: their deep pasts, colonial dispossession and recovery of rights.</p><p class="path-intro">Some peoples whose histories reach back thousands of years are living communities today. The ancestors of Aboriginal Australians reached Sahul 50,000 to 65,000 years ago, the Māori settled New Zealand around 1300, and the Lakota made the Black Hills their home by the 1770s. Most of these peoples met colonial rule, with killings, epidemics, forced removal and bans on their languages, and many have since won back land and recognition, from the Mabo Case in Australia to Nunavut and the Sámi parliaments. This path visits ten of them, in the order their histories begin.</p><p class="path-types">Suits <a href="/start">the Chronicler</a> · <a href="/start">the Timekeeper</a></p><p class="path-print-row"><button type="button" class="ws-print path-print">Print this path</button></p></div>
<ol class="path-steps"><li><a class="path-step" href="/culture/aboriginal-australians"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aboriginal-tasmanians.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">1 · Profile · c. 63000 BCE – present</span><span class="path-step-name">Aboriginal Australians</span><span class="path-step-why">Their ancestors reached Sahul 50,000 to 65,000 years ago; more than 400 peoples, each with its own language and Country, survived British colonisation from 1788.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Aboriginal Australians are the Indigenous peoples of mainland Australia and Tasmania, whose ancestors have lived on the continent for at least 50,000 years.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/culture/san-people"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/san-people.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">2 · Profile · c. 8000 BCE – present</span><span class="path-step-name">San people</span><span class="path-step-why">Descendants of hunter-gatherers who 10,000 years ago were spread from Tanzania to Cape Agulhas, the San faced a Dutch East India Company policy of eradication from 1777.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The San are indigenous hunter-gatherer peoples of southern Africa, living today mainly in Botswana, Namibia and South Africa and speaking click languages of several families.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/culture/sami"><span class="path-step-ph">3</span><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">3 · Profile · c. 1000 BCE – present</span><span class="path-step-name">Sámi</span><span class="path-step-why">In the far north of Europe since the Bronze Age or early Iron Age, the Sámi bore centuries of assimilation and have elected parliaments of their own since Finland’s of 1973.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Sámi are the indigenous people of the far north of Norway, Sweden, Finland and Russia's Kola Peninsula, a region they call Sápmi.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/culture/khoekhoe"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/transhumance.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">4 · Profile · c. 400 BCE – present</span><span class="path-step-name">Khoekhoe</span><span class="path-step-why">Herders of sheep and cattle since about the turn of the era, the Khoekhoe killed Francisco de Almeida at Salt River in 1510 and later lost their land to colonial conquest.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Khoekhoe, formerly called Khoikhoi, are the indigenous pastoral peoples of south-western Africa, who herded cattle and sheep and spoke click languages.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/culture/tuareg"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tuareg.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">5 · Profile · c. 400 CE – present</span><span class="path-step-name">Tuareg</span><span class="path-step-why">Amazigh herders and caravan traders of the Sahara with a founding queen, Tin Hinan, they resisted French conquest from 1881 to 1920 and saw their homeland divided among five states.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Tuareg are a Berber-speaking, traditionally nomadic people of the central and western Sahara and the Sahel.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/culture/inuit"><span class="path-step-ph">6</span><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">6 · Profile · c. 1000 CE – present</span><span class="path-step-name">Inuit</span><span class="path-step-why">Descendants of the Thule people who spread east from the Bering Strait around 1000 CE, the Inuit survived forced relocation and won the land claim that created Nunavut in 1999.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Inuit are the Indigenous peoples of the Arctic of Greenland, Canada and Alaska, speaking languages of the Eskimo-Aleut family.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/culture/ainu"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ainu.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">7 · Profile · c. 1200 CE – present</span><span class="path-step-name">Ainu</span><span class="path-step-why">The Indigenous people of Hokkaido, Sakhalin and the Kurils, whose culture took shape from the 13th century, lost land and language to Japanese colonisation and won legal recognition in 2019.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Ainu are an indigenous people of Hokkaido, Sakhalin and the Kuril Islands, with their own language, religion and culture centred on hunting, fishing and trade.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/culture/kalinago"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/garifuna.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">8 · Profile · c. 1200 CE – present</span><span class="path-step-name">Kalinago</span><span class="path-step-why">The Island Caribs kept their independence in the Lesser Antilles against Spain, France and England, and live today chiefly in the Kalinago Territory of Dominica.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Kalinago, also known as the Island Caribs, are an Indigenous people of the Lesser Antilles, who lived on islands such as Dominica, Saint Vincent, Guadeloupe and Martinique when Europeans arrived.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/culture/maori"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-waitangi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">9 · Profile · c. 1300 CE – present</span><span class="path-step-name">Māori</span><span class="path-step-why">Sailing from eastern Polynesia in waka, the Māori settled New Zealand around 1250–1350 and built a culture of iwi, pā and carved meeting houses before the Treaty of Waitangi of 1840.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/culture/lakota-people"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lakota-people.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">10 · Profile · c. 1600 CE – present</span><span class="path-step-name">Lakota people</span><span class="path-step-why">Bison hunters of the northern Plains who with the Cheyenne defeated Custer at the Little Bighorn in 1876, they still refuse the 1980 Supreme Court award for the Black Hills.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Lakota, or Teton Sioux, are the westernmost division of the Oceti Sakowin, made up of seven bands including the Oglala, Hunkpapa, Sicangu and Miniconjou.</span></span></a></li></ol>
